    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-e-lee">Lee</a> invaded the North in June 1863 partly for supplies, relief for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> and the hope of strengthening the Northern peace movement, and the armies met by chance at Gettysburg.</li><li>The Union army held a fishhook-shaped line on interior lines through three days and turned back Pickett's Charge on 3 July.</li><li>Historians agree it was a Union victory but are divided on whether it was decisive, and the war went on for almost two more years.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Only one civilian death is documented during the battle, Ginnie Wade, yet the town of 2,400 was left to care for 14,000 wounded Union troops and 8,000 Confederate prisoners.</p></aside>
    </section>

