    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-gettysburg" data-v="00f6f0c7c0" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>Heth's brigades meet Buford's cavalry west of town</b> Buford's cavalry held the ridges west of the town to delay the Confederates. Two of Heth's brigades, part of Hill's corps, came east along the Chambersburg Pike and met his dismounted troopers.</li><li><b>The I Corps arrives as the cavalry falls back</b> The Confederates pushed the Union cavalry east to McPherson Ridge. The vanguard of the I Corps then arrived and took up the fight beside it.</li><li><b>Davis and Archer are repelled on McPherson Ridge</b> North of the pike Davis won a brief success against Cutler's brigade but was repelled with heavy losses near an unfinished railroad cut. South of it Archer's brigade attacked through Herbst Woods and the Iron Brigade captured him.</li><li><b>Pettigrew and Pender drive the I Corps back</b> Heth's whole division engaged, with Pettigrew's brigade flanking the Union line, and Pender's division was added to the assault. The I Corps was driven back through the Seminary grounds and the streets of Gettysburg.</li><li><b>Ewell's corps and the XI Corps come up from the north</b> Two divisions of Ewell's corps came down on the town from the north while the XI Corps hurried up. Rodes's and Early's divisions then assaulted and outflanked the I and XI Corps north and northwest of the town.</li><li><b>The Union line falls back to Cemetery Hill</b> Howard ordered a retreat to Cemetery Hill, and the I Corps was driven back through the Seminary grounds and the streets of Gettysburg.</li><li><b>The Union line forms a fishhook</b> Most of the Union infantry reached the field. The XII Corps stood on Culp's Hill, the remnants of the I and XI Corps on Cemetery Hill and the II Corps on Cemetery Ridge. Longstreet's corps came up to attack the Union left.</li><li><b>Sickles's corps meets Longstreet's attack</b> Sickles had advanced the III Corps west to the Emmitsburg Road, away from Cemetery Ridge. Longstreet's divisions came forward and found it directly in their path.</li><li><b>Hood attacks Devil's Den and Little Round Top</b> Meade sent in the V Corps among other reinforcements. Hood's division moved further east than intended and attacked Devil's Den and Little Round Top.</li><li><b>McLaws overwhelms the III Corps at the Peach Orchard</b> McLaws, coming in on Hood's left, drove several attacks into the thinly stretched III Corps in the Wheatfield and overwhelmed it in the Peach Orchard. The III Corps was virtually destroyed as a combat unit.</li><li><b>The Pennsylvania Reserves beat McLaws back</b> McLaws's attack reached Plum Run Valley before the Pennsylvania Reserves division of the V Corps, coming down from Little Round Top, beat it back.</li><li><b>Pickett's Charge crosses the open ground</b> After a long bombardment, Pickett's division and brigades from Hill's corps stepped off the ridge and advanced to Cemetery Ridge. To the northeast Stuart's cavalry met Gregg's division and Custer's brigade, who stopped it reaching the rear of the Union infantry.</li><li><b>The charge is repelled at the Angle</b> The Union line wavered and broke for a time at the Angle, but reinforcements rushed into the breach and the Confederate attack was repelled. Nearly half of the attackers did not return to their own lines.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/robert-e-lee"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-e-lee.jpg" alt="Robert E. Lee" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/robert-e-lee">Robert E. Lee</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Robert_Edward_Lee_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Julian Vannerson, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Battle of Gettysburg was fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a> between the Union and Confederate armies, in and around Gettysburg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/army-of-northern-virginia">Army of Northern Virginia</a> defeated the Army of the Potomac at Chancellorsville (30 April to 6 May 1863), General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-e-lee">Robert E. Lee</a> decided on a second invasion of the North. Such a move would upset the Union's summer plans, possibly ease the pressure on the besieged Confederate garrison at Vicksburg, let the army live off northern farms while war-ravaged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> rested, and possibly strengthen the growing peace movement in the North.</p><p>Lee's army began to move north from Fredericksburg on 3 June. After the death of Thomas J. "Stonewall" Jackson he had reorganised it into three corps under James Longstreet, Richard S. Ewell and A. P. Hill. Lee ordered his army to limit its effect on civilians, but Northern farmers and merchants were paid in virtually worthless Confederate money, and towns such as York were required to pay indemnities under threat of destruction. During the invasion the Confederates seized between 40 and nearly 60 northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a>; a few were escaped fugitives, but many were freemen, and all were sent south into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> under guard.</p><p>Lee let J. E. B. Stuart take part of the cavalry around the Union army, and Stuart was absent from the army through the approach and the first two days of the battle. On 28 June Lincoln replaced Joseph Hooker with George Gordon Meade. On 1 July two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">brigades</a> of Henry Heth's division advanced on Gettysburg, in what A. P. Hill meant as a reconnaissance in force.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Union Army of the Potomac, under Hooker until 28 June and then Meade, numbered more than 100,000 men. It had seven infantry corps (I, II, III, V, VI, XI and XII), a Cavalry Corps under Alfred Pleasonton and an artillery reserve; Brigadier General Henry Jackson Hunt was chief of artillery on Meade's staff. John Buford's cavalry division was the first Union force to hold the ground west of Gettysburg, fighting dismounted with breech-loading Sharps carbines. A small minority of historians write that some troopers had Spencer repeating weapons, but most sources disagree.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-e-lee">Lee</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/army-of-northern-virginia">Army of Northern Virginia</a> is given as 75,000 men in one passage of the source and 72,000 in another. Its three infantry corps were led by Longstreet (First), Ewell (Second) and Hill (Third), and Stuart commanded the cavalry division. Ewell and Hill, who had been division commanders under Jackson, were new to corps command.</p><p>On the first day about 22,000 of Meade's men and 27,000 of Lee's were engaged. By 2 July most of both armies had arrived. The Union line took the shape of a "fishhook" from Culp's Hill through Cemetery Hill and along Cemetery Ridge to just north of Little Round Top. The Confederate line ran about a mile to the west on Seminary Ridge and then through the town and round to a point opposite Culp's Hill, so that it was nearly five miles (8 km) long; the Union army held interior lines. Lee's plan for 2 July was a general assault, with Longstreet to attack the Union left and Ewell to attack Culp's Hill and Cemetery Hill when he heard Longstreet's guns.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>On 1 July Buford laid out his defence on three ridges west of the town. About 7:30 a.m. Heth's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">brigades</a> met his cavalry three miles west, and by 10:20 a.m. the Confederates had pushed it back to McPherson Ridge, where the vanguard of John F. Reynolds's I Corps arrived. In the afternoon Robert E. Rodes's and Jubal Early's divisions of Ewell's corps struck the Union I and XI corps from the north and north-west, and both corps retreated through the town to Cemetery Hill. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-e-lee">Lee</a> had told Ewell to take Cemetery Hill "if practicable"; Ewell judged an assault not practicable and did not attempt it.</p><p>On 2 July Lee's attack on the Union left began late. Daniel Sickles had moved his III Corps forward to the Peach Orchard against orders, creating a salient. Hood's and McLaws's divisions attacked just after 4 p.m. and 5 p.m., and there was fighting at Devil's Den, the Wheatfield and Little Round Top. Meade sent 20,000 reinforcements, and McLaws's attack was beaten back. About six o'clock Ewell's divisions attacked Culp's Hill and East Cemetery Hill and did not break the line.</p><p>On 3 July the second fight for Culp's Hill ended about 11 a.m.. Around 1 p.m. 150 to 170 Confederate guns began a bombardment, probably the largest of the war. About 3 p.m. between 10,500 and 12,500 Confederate soldiers advanced towards Cemetery Ridge in what is called Pickett's Charge. Fire from artillery and from Hancock's II Corps met them. The line broke briefly at the Angle and was restored. Nearly half the attackers did not return, and Pickett's division lost about two-thirds of its men.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ulysses-s-grant"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ulysses-s-grant.jpg" alt="Ulysses S. Grant" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ulysses-s-grant">Ulysses S. Grant</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ulysses_S._Grant_1870-1880_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Brady-Handy Photograph Collection, Library of Congress, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The two armies suffered between 46,000 and 51,000 casualties. Union casualties were 23,055 (3,155 killed, 14,531 wounded, 5,369 captured or missing). Many authors give as many as 28,000 for the Confederates; Busey and Martin (2005) document 23,231 (4,708 killed, 12,693 wounded, 5,830 captured or missing). Nearly a third of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-e-lee">Lee</a>'s general officers were killed, wounded or captured. Between 3,000 and 5,000 horses were killed.</p><p>Only one civilian death during the battle is documented: Ginnie Wade, 20, hit by a stray bullet in her kitchen. The town, with a population of 2,400, was left to care for 14,000 wounded Union troops and an additional 8,000 Confederate prisoners. Nearly 8,000 dead needed burying quickly in the summer heat, and more than 3,000 horse carcasses were burned south of the town, which made townspeople violently ill.</p><p>On 4 July, the day the Vicksburg garrison surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulysses-s-grant">Ulysses S. Grant</a>, Meade did not attack, and Lee's proposal for a prisoner exchange was rejected. Lee began to withdraw that afternoon, with a wagon train seventeen miles long carrying supplies and wounded men. At Monterey Pass on 4 July Union cavalry took 150 to 300 wagons and 1,300 to 1,500 prisoners. Swollen water and a destroyed pontoon bridge held the Confederates at the Potomac until the night of 13 July. Meade's infantry did not fully pursue until 7 July, and Lincoln and Halleck urged a harder pursuit. Lee's army crossed on 14 July, when Union cavalry took 500 prisoners and J. Johnston Pettigrew was mortally wounded.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Gettysburg&#8221; cites where it covers Casualties, Gettysburg Address:</p><ul><li>Sean Conant, The Gettysburg Address: Perspectives on Lincoln's Greatest Speech (Oxford University Press, 2015)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Much of what is told about the battle rests on the accounts of those who took part. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-e-lee">Lee</a> wrote an official report; Longstreet and Heth left memoirs; and Ewell claimed years later that Lee had changed his orders for 2 July, though this appears in neither his own nor Lee's official report. Longstreet's memoir gives his own account of what he told Lee on 3 July. Heth's memoir says that he sent Pettigrew into the town to look for supplies, especially shoes. Works on the battle include The Bachelder Papers (subtitled Gettysburg in Their Own Words), the Fremantle Diary, Haskell's account and Frassanito's study of early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/photography">photography</a> at the field, and the Official Records are available online.</p><p>Numbers are disputed. Union losses are given as 23,055, but Confederate losses are harder to establish: many authors give as many as 28,000, while Busey and Martin document 23,231. Stephen Sears puts the casualties of the whole six-week campaign at 57,225.</p><p>The causes of Lee's defeat are partisan ground. The dominant theme of the Lost Cause writers was that Lee's senior generals failed him, and they blamed his generals, Longstreet most of all, who criticised Lee in print and became a Republican after the war. Other historians hold that Lee did not manage his subordinates adequately, and most modern historians agree that both Lee and Stuart bear some responsibility for the cavalry's absence. Many historians speculate that Jackson would have taken Culp's Hill on 1 July. Whether the battle was decisive is also disputed (see Legacy).</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The news electrified the North; The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> Inquirer ran the headline "Victory! <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Waterloo</a> Eclipsed!" Enthusiasm faded when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-e-lee">Lee</a>'s army escaped, and Lincoln and others criticised Meade's pursuit. Lincoln refused passage to Confederate Vice President Alexander Stephens, who approached the lines under a flag of truce, and Henry Adams wrote that all idea of European intervention was at an end. In the South Gettysburg was at first seen as a setback, not a disaster.</p><p>Historians are divided on whether the battle was decisive. Its reputation as the "turning point", usually coupled with the fall of Vicksburg, rests on the observation that Lee's army conducted no more strategic offensives. James M. McPherson wrote that Lee's army never again had the power and reputation it carried into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>. Herman Hattaway and Archer Jones judged the strategic impact "fairly limited", and Alan Guelzo notes that the war lasted two more years. Thomas Goss and John Keegan doubt the label "decisive battle".</p><p>On 19 November 1863 Lincoln spoke at the dedication of the Soldiers' National Cemetery, later Gettysburg National Cemetery; his Gettysburg Address, of 271 words, is widely considered one of the most famous speeches in American history. Of 72 Medals of Honor for the campaign, 64 were for the battle; the latest went posthumously to Alonzo Cushing in 2014. At the 50th anniversary reunion in 1913 at least 35,000 Union veterans and a few more than 7,000 Confederate veterans attended, according to Carol Reardon, and some re-enacted Pickett's Charge in a spirit of reconciliation.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/nearer-my-god-to-thee">Nearer, My God, to Thee<span>1841</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At Gettysburg in July 1863 the Union Army of the Potomac stopped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-e-lee">Robert E. Lee</a>'s second invasion of the North after three days of fighting and some 46,000 to 51,000 casualties on both sides. Historians still dispute how decisive it was.</em></p>

